Z||000000||LAMBO||01-16-2001||09:40 AM||||I have an 84, no sunroof car that has a perfectly clean headliner in it. The only problem is that it has a few "bubbles" in it, I guess from age. Does anyone know of any glue application that could be injected through the fabric, onto the foam above, so that I don't have to completley change the fabric. Yes, I could be accused of being partially lazy, but the fabric is in perfect condition. Just trying to salvage what is already there.<BR>Todd||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||01-16-2001||10:04 AM||||I had a friend with an 85 that he just took the trim off and used the spray-on glue. To get into it without tearing it apart, he used a nozzle with a tube. I don't know if this will work for you but it's a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||01-16-2001||10:38 AM||||It is a waste of time. It is not the glue that is letting go but the foam backing that is deteriating.  You might fix one place and tomorrow another will let go. Just remove the entire headliner and recover it. It will cost only about $25 or less for headliner material and glue.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ponya||01-18-2001||10:51 PM||||skitime is right. I just had mine done by a guy that does convertible tops etc. I cleaned off the headliner and for $30.00 he used manufacturer's weight material and professional glue that they sprayed on. Also got a 3 year guarantee. Looks great and it's done professionally and it only took 1/2 hour.   Bob||||reg||4
